in this video I'll be introducing the  rational numbers and set builder  notation the rational numbers are  defined as a ratio of two integers and  examples include the integers themselves  terminating decimals and repeating  decimals so let's start with repeating  decimals if we let 10x equal nine point  nine nine nine repeating then if we  divide by both sides by 10 then x equals  zero point nine nine nine repeating and  if we subtract X from 10x we have 9x  equals nine and then divide both sides  by nine we have x equals one and then if  we multiply by ten both sides by 10 we  have 10x equals 10 so now we have 10x  equals 10  but 10x also equals nine point nine nine  and repeating so we have now and these  are in fact true nine point nine nine  repeating equals ten so we think we can  now express 10x as an integer which is a  rational number we can put 10 divided by  one which is a ratio so another problem  we have is terminating decimals if we  have two point seven eight equal to Y  then we can just easily say 278 divided  by 100 equals y as well these are two  integers and it's a it's another  rational number  so a convenient way to define these is  denoted with a Q and this is called set  builder notation we have the Q equals  the set containing elements a divided by  B such that a and B are elements of the  integers when the only condition that B  cannot equal 0 if you divide by zero its  its undefined it doesn't mean anything  so if you're interested in that I  recommend googling it I think it's  interesting
 

